WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.443
Film Courage: Having gone through the experience
of meeting Jen [his film’s screenwriter]

00:00:02.443 --> 00:00:08.130
and going to this pitch fest, what advice
can you give other screenwriters on meeting

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.920
a filmmaker and pitching their script?

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.220
Jay Silverman, photographer and director of
OFF THE MENU: You know, it’s no different

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.720
than being an photographer or a director.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:20.080
You don’t have an opportunity to explain
yourself, it’s either on the page or it’s

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.080
not on the page.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.240
Now if I had met Jen [Goldson, the writer
of Jay’s film OFF THE MENU] and she was

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:29.100
quote-unquote “difficult” just at the
get-go, I probably would have never even read

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.100
the script.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:35.320
So I think having a little bit of communication
skills is a gift in that regard.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:44.010
I also think stereotypically I’m not like
the stereotype, I’m open to collaborating.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:48.719
And if you find out right at the beginning
that the person isn’t…here’s a great

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:55.030
example a woman who…Bethany [Cerrona, Jay’s
producer] found me a wonderful script about

00:00:55.030 --> 00:01:06.090
hate and the challenge of working with groups
that hate and it was a really, really well-written

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:07.090
movie.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:16.399
It was also a play and I had wished in hindsight
that I didn’t spend an hour on the phone

00:01:16.399 --> 00:01:21.310
with the writer when they said “Oh, one
more thing I forgot to tell you is I have

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:22.590
to direct this myself.”

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:28.229
So I felt that this was something that should
have come out in the first ten seconds because

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:34.920
to me it’s not uncommon for a screenwriter
to say “I don’t want anybody else to direct

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.029
this but me.”

00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:41.640
I only produce movies that I direct and the
outcome of it is that is I’ve learned that

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:42.919
lesson now.

00:01:42.919 --> 00:01:49.810
Film Courage: So if that is a requirement
or special requirements like I want very little

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.380
edits done, get this out right away?

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:53.670
Jay: I would pass.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.689
I mean, it’s just not worth it.

00:01:55.689 --> 00:02:04.359
There’s too many…the independent film
business is so convoluted with waste and waste

00:02:04.359 --> 00:02:11.159
and then when you finally get to the execution…here’s
a wonderful example, my other producer Will

00:02:11.159 --> 00:02:20.760
Newman who helped produce this movie, he was
not just an incredible collaborator to this

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:24.439
movie but he was the hatchet man in a positive
way.

00:02:24.439 --> 00:02:30.930
He would come in and say, no disrespect to
Jen’s material but she’s got 56 characters

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:36.370
and he knew right away…bingo…that that
was going to translate into a portion of the

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:39.690
budget that was disproportionate with what
we had.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.640
And what does that mean in English?

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:47.490
The only affordable amount of characters we
could have in his opinion was between 20 and

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:48.490
25 characters.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:53.160
And I know that sounds crazy but any independent
filmmaker is going to come in front of the

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.320
same moment that I came in front of when you
go “Do you really need that character to

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.469
walk in the door and go Hey, you’ve got
a phone call.”

00:02:59.469 --> 00:03:05.300
That character is a SAG talking actor and
that’s going to cost you X amount of dollars.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:09.109
And you put 40 more of those in the movie
and you’ve made a movie that you’re not

00:03:09.109 --> 00:03:13.730
going to be able to afford and it’s crazy
and I know that you are interviewing me about

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:18.950
creativity but if you can get this stuff off
the table before you start talking creativity,

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:23.049
you’ll have (in my opinion) a more controllable
project.

